  "worked_solution": "To compute the position vector, we need to integrate the velocity vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =\\int v(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int \u27e86t-1,{t}^{2}-1\u27e9\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\u27e8\\int (6t-1)\\,\\text{d}t,\\int ({t}^{2}-1)\\,\\text{d}t\u27e9 \\\\  & =\u27e83{t}^{2}-t+{C}_{1},\\frac{{t}^{3}}{3}-t+{C}_{2}\u27e9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can solve for the constants of integration using the fact that $r(3)=\u27e810,-1\u27e9.$ Substituting $t=3,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(3) & =\u27e83({3}^{2})-3+{C}_{1},\\frac{{3}^{3}}{3}-3+{C}_{2}\u27e9 \\\\ \u27e810,-1\u27e9 & =\u27e824+{C}_{1},6+{C}_{2}\u27e9,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso we must have ${C}_{1}=-14$ and ${C}_{2}=-7.$ Therefore, the position vector at time $t$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =\u27e83{t}^{2}-t-14,\\frac{{t}^{3}}{3}-t-7\u27e9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the position vector at time $t=1,$ we substitute $t=1$ into the above, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(1) & =\u27e83({1}^{2})-1-14,\\frac{{1}^{3}}{3}-1-7\u27e9 \\\\  & =\u27e8-12,-\\frac{23}{3}\u27e9.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
